id: ops-setc
title: "Attributing a case arm label by presence rather than position refused an attested publish, and a quoted key hid a phantom one"
description: "Two more fail modes in the canonical attestation gate, both reported by review on this branch and both confirmed by executing the auditor rather than by reading it. They are the tenth and eleventh found here, and the tenth is again a fix for an earlier one over-correcting.\n\nThe first is a fail-closed introduced by the nested-case fix. That fix asked whether a segment starts a case arm while a case is already open, which is true both of a later sibling arm and of the first arm of a nested case the same segment opens. In the one-segment form, where the arm label, the assignment, the nested opener and the publish share a line, the reset fired on the nested first arm and discarded a binding the enclosing arm had legitimately made, refusing a publish the shell does attest. Position separates the two: a label before the opener belongs to the enclosing case, a label after it belongs to the block this segment opens.\n\nThe second is a fail-open from having two matchers that had to agree. A quoted YAML key matched the run header pattern but not the generic block scalar pattern, so a non-run block with a quoted key was never consumed and an inner run block inside it was read as executable, admitting a phantom publish that satisfied the non-vacuity guard. Recognising quoted keys in only the generic matcher would have inverted the bug rather than fixed it, hiding a real publish written under a quoted run key. The two matchers are now one, with the key deciding executability."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: "area:gates","area:supply-chain","type:security"
created_at: "2026-09-02T18:38:27.455Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-02T18:38:28.527Z"
